/**
\page org_mitk_views_remeshing The Remeshing View

\imageMacro{RemeshingIcon.png,"Icon of the Remeshing View",2.00}

\tableofcontents

\section org_mitk_views_remeshingOverview Overview

The Remeshing view rebuilds a surface from a new set of evenly shaped triangles that stays close to the original shape.
Its most common use is to reduce surfaces that are far denser than they need to be, such as surfaces created from segmentations, to a fraction of their vertices.
At the same time, it replaces long and thin triangles by nearly equilateral ones, which suits further processing like simulations.

Remeshing is based on <a href="https://github.com/valette/ACVD">ACVD</a>, which groups the vertices of the input into evenly sized patches and places one vertex of the result in each patch.
See S. Valette and J.-M. Chassery, "Approximated Centroidal Voronoi Diagrams for Uniform Polygonal Mesh Coarsening", Computer Graphics Forum 23(3), 2004.

\section org_mitk_views_remeshingUsage Remeshing a surface

<ol>
  <li> Select the surface to remesh.
  <li> Choose between \ref org_mitk_views_remeshingMode "Adaptive and Regular" remeshing.
  <li> Choose a \ref org_mitk_views_remeshingQuality "quality" and a \ref org_mitk_views_remeshingDensity "density", and check that the memory it needs is available.
  <li> Adjust the \ref org_mitk_views_remeshingAdvanced "advanced options" if the defaults do not fit.
  <li> Click <b>Remesh</b>.
</ol>

Hovering over a setting shows a short explanation of it below the <b>Remesh</b> button.

\section org_mitk_views_remeshingSettings Settings

\subsection org_mitk_views_remeshingMode Adaptive and Regular remeshing

<b>Adaptive</b> remeshing places more vertices where the surface bends and fewer where it is flat.
It keeps fine details even at a low density, at the cost of somewhat less uniform triangles around strong bends.

<b>Regular</b> remeshing spreads the vertices evenly regardless of the shape.
It gives the most uniform triangles and is the better choice for smooth surfaces and for results that are processed further.

\subsection org_mitk_views_remeshingQuality Quality

To place its vertices well, remeshing needs many more vertices in the input than it produces.
When the input has too few, it is refined first, and the higher quality settings refine it further.
This makes the triangles of the result a little more uniform, but multiplies computation time and memory.

<b>High</b> suits nearly every surface.
<b>Very high</b> and <b>Maximum</b> are for the rare cases in which the last bit of uniformity matters.

\subsection org_mitk_views_remeshingDensity Density

The density sets the number of vertices of the result relative to the input: 50% halves it, and surfaces that are much denser than necessary often do well with 1-10%.
The result has at least 100 vertices.

The bar below the slider shows how much memory remeshing needs at each density, compared to the memory currently available:

<ul>
  <li> <b>Green:</b> up to half of the available memory.
  <li> <b>Orange:</b> up to 85% of it.
  <li> <b>Red:</b> more than that. Remeshing may fail or slow down the whole system.
</ul>

The text below the bar gives the estimated share for the current density, and turns red as well.

Memory grows in steps rather than with every percent of density, because refining the input quadruples its number of vertices each time.
The steps lie at about 10% and 40% for High, 2%, 8% and 32% for Very high, and 3.2%, 12.8% and 51.2% for Maximum.
A density just above a step therefore needs two and a half to four times the memory of a density just below it.

\subsection org_mitk_views_remeshingAdvanced Advanced options

<ul>
  <li> <b>Replace original surface:</b> the remeshed surface takes the place of the original in its node, which keeps its name, color, opacity, all other properties and its place in the Data Manager.
       The original surface is gone afterwards.
  <li> <b>Hide original surface:</b> once the remeshed surface is added, the original is hidden so that it does not cover the result.
       This option does not apply when the original is replaced.
  <li> <b>Show remeshed surface as wireframe:</b> shows the edges of the triangles of the result instead of a closed surface.
</ul>

By default, the result is added as a new node, shown as wireframe, and the original is hidden.

\section org_mitk_views_remeshingRunning While remeshing runs

Remeshing runs in the background, so MITK stays usable meanwhile.
The settings of the view are locked until it is done.

<ul>
  <li> A notification in the bottom right corner of the window shows that remeshing is running.
       It has no percentage, since how long remeshing takes depends too much on the shape of the surface to be predicted.
       Its <b>cancel</b> button stops the remeshing.
  <li> The original surface pulses in the 3D view until the result is ready.
  <li> Closing the view cancels the remeshing.
</ul>

Unless the original is replaced, the result is added as a child of the original surface, in the color of the original.
It is named after the original with "-remeshed" appended, followed by "-2", "-3" and so on if the original already has a child of that name.
If the original was removed while remeshing ran, the result is added at the top level of the Data Manager.

\section org_mitk_views_remeshingNotes Notes

<ul>
  <li> Surfaces made of polygons other than triangles are triangulated first.
       Triangle strips, lines and single points are ignored.
  <li> Of a surface with several time steps, only the first one is remeshed.
       Replacing such a surface leaves it with that single time step.
  <li> Open surfaces may shrink slightly and unevenly along their borders.
  <li> Remeshing the same surface twice gives slightly different, equally good results.
  <li> Remeshing with more memory than is available can fail, usually with a message, and in the worst case end MITK.
       Keep the memory bar out of the red, choose a lower density or quality, or close other applications.
</ul>
*/
